import{b as t,I as r,g as o,j as a}from"./chunks/framework.CrSplIeq.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Overview","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/overview.md","filePath":"guide/overview.md"}'),s={name:"guide/overview.md"};function n(i,e,d,l,c,h){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="overview" tabindex="-1">Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Overview&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Gatelin is a <strong>Backend for Frontend (BFF)</strong>: the application’s API layer. Put it <strong>behind</strong> an edge reverse proxy (Traefik, nginx, Envoy). That proxy is the public gateway (TLS, host routing, load balancing). Gatelin is this app’s front door: sessions, RBAC, and authorized forwarding to its microservices.</p><p>It is not a cluster ingress, a service mesh, or a multi-tenant traffic gateway. Control-plane URLs stay under <code>/gatelin/*</code>; that prefix is the BFF’s own API, not an edge product name.</p><p>Gatelin handles:</p><ul><li>🛣️ <strong>Transparent HTTP proxy</strong> — Dynamic route matching with streamed request/response bodies, headers, status codes, multipart, and binary payloads</li><li>📡 <strong>Realtime protocols</strong> — Server-Sent Events and authenticated WebSocket upgrades; GraphQL queries and mutations pass through as ordinary HTTP</li><li>👤 <strong>Consumer management</strong> — With automatic token refresh</li><li>🗺️ <strong>Routes management</strong> — Organize and control available API endpoints</li><li>🛎️ <strong>Services management</strong> — Register, update, and monitor backend services</li><li>🌐 <strong>CORS management</strong> — Configure and enforce Cross-Origin Resource Sharing policies</li><li>🔐 <strong>Authentication</strong> — JWT token validation, consumer session management, and mid-login challenges (2FA / expired password) delegated to the password service</li><li>🛡️ <strong>Authorization</strong> — Role-based access control (ACL) validation per route, per property, per condition</li><li>🎭 <strong>Role management</strong> — Create, update, archive, and search roles with assigned permissions</li><li>🔑 <strong>Permission management</strong> — Per-role, per-route operation access stored in Gatelin’s database</li><li>🎛️ <strong>Front-end admin</strong> — Manage sessions, routes, roles, and ACL from a web interface</li></ul><h2 id="key-concepts" tabindex="-1">Key Concepts <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-concepts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key Concepts&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="routes" tabindex="-1">Routes <a class="header-anchor" href="#routes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Routes&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>route</strong> is the mapping between an incoming URL pattern and a backend microservice. Each route combines:</p><ul><li>a <strong>URL pattern</strong> to match against incoming requests,</li><li>a <strong>target service</strong> and <strong>resource</strong> that will handle the request,</li><li>a list of allowed <strong>HTTP operations</strong> (e.g. GET, POST, PUT).</li></ul><p>When a request arrives, Gatelin matches its URL against the registered routes to determine which microservice to forward it to.</p><h3 id="consumers" tabindex="-1">Consumers <a class="header-anchor" href="#consumers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consumers&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>consumer</strong> represents an authenticated user session. It is identified by a pair of JWT tokens (access token and refresh token) and linked to a user account from the user microservice. Each consumer carries an array of role IDs that determine what they are authorized to do.</p><p>Login may be interrupted by a <strong>mid-login challenge</strong> when the password service reports an expired password or enabled 2FA. In that case <code>POST /gatelin/sessions</code> returns <strong>202</strong> with a challenge URL; after the user completes the workflow, the frontend redeems a one-shot ticket via <code>POST /gatelin/sessions/resume</code> to create the consumer. See <a href="./api-sessions.html">Sessions</a>.</p><p>Consumers are loaded into an <strong>in-memory cache</strong> at startup and kept up to date on every token refresh. This makes authentication lookups fast without hitting the database on every request.</p><h3 id="roles" tabindex="-1">Roles <a class="header-anchor" href="#roles" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Roles&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>role</strong> is a named group of permissions, scoped to an application. Consumers are assigned one or more roles, and those roles define what they are allowed to access. Roles can be archived, and carry a name, description, and display color for administration purposes.</p><h3 id="permissions" tabindex="-1">Permissions <a class="header-anchor" href="#permissions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Permissions&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <strong>permission</strong> is the explicit grant that links a <strong>role</strong> to a <strong>route</strong> and an <strong>operation</strong> (e.g. <code>GET /users</code>). It may also restrict <strong>fields</strong> (writable/readable columns), <strong>scopes</strong> (URL sub-segments), and <strong>conditions</strong> (row-level filters). On every incoming request, Gatelin checks whether the consumer&#39;s roles include a permission that covers the matched route and HTTP method. If no matching permission exists, the request is rejected with a 403 error.</p><p>When a consumer has multiple roles, their permissions are merged.</p><h3 id="proxy-authorization-contract" tabindex="-1">Proxy authorization contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#proxy-authorization-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Proxy authorization contract&quot;">​</a></h3><p>For Gatelin&#39;s own <code>/gatelin/*</code> APIs, field allow-lists and row conditions are enforced directly. For transparent proxied traffic, Gatelin enforces the route, operation, scope, and permission, then forwards resolved field and condition restrictions as <code>x-acl-fields</code> and <code>x-acl-conditions</code>. The upstream service must enforce those restrictions on reads and writes.</p><p>GraphQL over HTTP and SSE use the same request-level contract. WebSocket is authorized once at the upgrade handshake; messages after the upgrade are opaque. Native gRPC is delegated to Traefik or Envoy rather than proxied by Gatelin.</p>',22)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
